nt", "Allen's huge fundraising edge ($1.3M cash vs. $3.5K for Smith)", "Unanimous expert ratings from Solid R to Safe R", "Markets price Allen near 90% to win"], "rationale": "Rick Allen is a well-funded incumbent in a district that leans Republican by seven points, and every major rater places the race at Solid or Safe R while markets give Smith only about a 10% chance. Smith's minimal fundraising leaves her little ability to change the race's fundamentals.", "watch": "A major national Democratic wave or outside spending flooding the district could narrow the margin, though an upset remains unlikely.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Rick Allen (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Rick Allen, the Republican incumbent, seeks another term in a Georgia district that leans Republican by about seven points. He won renomination in May and holds a commanding financial advantage, with roughly $1.3 million on hand compared to a few thousand dollars for Democratic nominee Ceretta Smith, who won her primary runoff in June.\n\nEvery expert rating places the race at Solid or Safe Republican, and prediction markets give Allen roughly a 90 percent chance of winning. With no public polling and little outside spending, the race has drawn little competitive attention, and Allen's committee activity and constituent events suggest a standard incumbent campaign. Smith would need a dramatic shift in the national environment to contend.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0633, "margin": -10.05}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0229, "margin": -14.0}]}